sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Computer simulation is not merely a convenient substitute for mathematical formulas: there are many common systems whose behavior can be determined only by something like explicit simulation, and there can be no easy theory for almost any behavior that seems complex.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Many common systems can only be understood by explicit simulation";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Computational Irreducibility” (from p. 737), p. 742. Scope: common natural and artificial systems with complex behavior"
}
